WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.050
MARTIN OMANDER: Hello.

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:04.380
I'm Martin Omander, a Developer
Advocate on the Serverless

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:05.215
team.

00:00:05.215 --> 00:00:07.590
CRAIG LABENZ: And I'm Craig
Labenz, a Developer Relations

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:10.500
Engineer on the Cloud SQL team.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:12.540
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh,
Cloud SQL, you say?

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:14.520
That's perfect
because I'm looking

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:17.400
to deploy my app on Cloud
Run, but it's back end

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:19.140
uses a relational database.

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:21.973
And I'm not sure how
to combine the two.

00:00:21.973 --> 00:00:22.890
CRAIG LABENZ: Ah, yes.

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:25.320
Many developers are using
serverless architectures

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:26.888
with relational databases.

00:00:26.888 --> 00:00:28.680
Why don't you show me
what you're building,

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:30.840
and we can take it from there?

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:32.348
MARTIN OMANDER: Sounds good.

00:00:32.348 --> 00:00:35.764
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:42.610 --> 00:00:44.830
All right, Craig,
I've decided to settle

00:00:44.830 --> 00:00:49.060
one of the greatest programming
head scratchers of all time.

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:52.510
CRAIG LABENZ: Ah,
GIF versus "jif?"

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:55.640
MARTIN OMANDER:
No, not that one.

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:57.527
CRAIG LABENZ: What
color is the dress?

00:00:57.527 --> 00:00:58.610
MARTIN OMANDER: No, Craig.

00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:00.260
I said programming.

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:03.200
I created a web app to poll
whether developers prefer

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:05.992
tabs or spaces in their code.

00:01:05.992 --> 00:01:08.450
CRAIG LABENZ: Oh, I love that
idea, because the answer will

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:10.880
obviously be [BLEEP].

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:12.260
Now, your app is
definitely going

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:14.690
to get millions of daily
active users from day one,

00:01:14.690 --> 00:01:17.550
so you'll need to be thoughtful
with its infrastructure.

00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:20.370
Can you show me what you have?

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:23.190
MARTIN OMANDER: Yep, my
app's code is ready to go.

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:25.620
There's a simple front
end that displays the most

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:27.960
recent votes plus the totals.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:31.920
Also, there's a form at the
bottom that collects new votes.

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:34.550
CRAIG LABENZ: OK, that sounds
like a pretty standard web app.

00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:37.200
Now, you mentioned using
SQL for its back end.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.800
What went into that decision?

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:42.050
MARTIN OMANDER: Well, I plan
to have several Cloud Run

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:45.500
services that periodically
run analysis on my data.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:48.050
And SQL is really
good at aggregations

00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:49.880
like sums and averages.

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:51.870
CRAIG LABENZ: Hmm,
yeah, that makes sense.

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:54.110
It sounds like Cloud
SQL is actually

00:01:54.110 --> 00:01:56.410
the right choice for your app.

00:01:56.410 --> 00:01:58.420
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh,
that's good to hear.

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:01.390
But my fear using SQL with
a serverless architecture

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:04.060
is that Postgres
and MySQL databases,

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:05.980
that they don't scale
up automatically

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:07.595
like Cloud Functions
or Cloud Run.

00:02:07.595 --> 00:02:09.220
CRAIG LABENZ: Oh,
we've all been there.

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:11.080
Now, luckily,
traditional SQL databases

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:12.910
have a lot of tricks
you can use to squeeze

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:15.580
every last drop of performance
out of your hardware.

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:17.140
What do you say we
deploy your app,

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:19.583
and then we'll run
some load tests?

00:02:19.583 --> 00:02:20.750
MARTIN OMANDER: Sounds good.

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:22.910
Let's get this baby shipped.

00:02:22.910 --> 00:02:26.690
All right, so I'm ready to
create my database instance

00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:28.940
here in Cloud SQL.

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:32.060
I click Create Instance.

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:35.150
And ah, the first thing, it
asks me for database engine.

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:37.340
Do you have any recommendation?

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:40.040
CRAIG LABENZ: Well, all three
provide a modern SQL database,

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:43.130
but the community seems to
be agreeing on Postgres-style

00:02:43.130 --> 00:02:44.790
SQL for new products.

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:46.860
So looking ahead,
choosing Postgres

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:49.790
will set you up nicely for
upgrades in the future.

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:52.040
But of course, every
decision that we make here

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:55.647
will be the same for
MySQL or SQL Server.

00:02:55.647 --> 00:02:56.730
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:02:56.730 --> 00:02:59.930
So I'll call my
instance poll-database.

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:03.350
There we go.

00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:07.100
And I'll enter a super
secret password here.

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:10.970
Let's see, secretpassword.

00:03:10.970 --> 00:03:12.890
All right.

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:15.980
Now, regarding location,
I'm in California,

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:21.470
so I'm physically closest
to, let's see, ah, US West.

00:03:21.470 --> 00:03:23.900
But to make sure I have the
same experience as users

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:26.690
far from California,
I think I'll deploy

00:03:26.690 --> 00:03:31.292
my app to maybe Europe North.

00:03:31.292 --> 00:03:32.750
CRAIG LABENZ: Oh,
that's so clever.

00:03:32.750 --> 00:03:35.900
By not deploying your app
to the nearest region,

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:38.780
you'll be able to see if
users far from your servers

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:41.968
have degraded experiences.

00:03:41.968 --> 00:03:43.510
MARTIN OMANDER:
Yeah, and, of course,

00:03:43.510 --> 00:03:46.120
if I know that all my
users are in one place,

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:49.000
I would deploy my
app to that region.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:50.650
But I have a question, Craig.

00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:52.630
I put this in Finland now.

00:03:52.630 --> 00:03:57.345
But can I then relocate my
Cloud SQL instance later on?

00:03:57.345 --> 00:03:58.720
CRAIG LABENZ: Oh,
yes, of course.

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:00.400
Moving your database
location is as

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:03.340
easy as creating a read
replica in your new region,

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:05.860
waiting for it to catch
up to your main database,

00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:08.250
and then promoting it.

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:09.630
MARTIN OMANDER: All right, cool.

00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:11.700
Next one is database version.

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:14.340
I'll pick the latest available.

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:17.519
Configuration options,
it says there.

00:04:17.519 --> 00:04:20.092
Oh, there is a lot here.

00:04:20.092 --> 00:04:21.300
CRAIG LABENZ: Yeah, there is.

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:23.160
Luckily, they're not too bad.

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:25.260
The first item,
Connectivity, offers a lot

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:26.670
of different ways
of establishing

00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:28.560
a secure connection
to your database.

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:30.780
The Private IP option
allows you to connect

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:32.280
to a virtual private cloud.

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:34.420
But I don't recommend
that for newer apps.

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:36.420
MARTIN OMANDER: OK, yeah,
I want to start simple

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:38.010
but in a way that
doesn't prevent me

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:39.542
from growing in the future.

00:04:39.542 --> 00:04:40.500
CRAIG LABENZ: For sure.

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:43.230
And you can always add a virtual
private cloud down the line

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:44.230
should you desire.

00:04:44.230 --> 00:04:46.710
So this means the default
connectivity options should

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:48.810
be exactly what you
want, because Cloud Run

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:52.532
is designed to connect to
Cloud SQL right out of the box.

00:04:52.532 --> 00:04:54.240
MARTIN OMANDER: Ah,
I love it when things

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:56.250
work right out of the box.

00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:58.860
OK, so closing connectivity.

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:01.650
I'm opening machine
type and storage.

00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:02.730
What should I pick here?

00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:03.630
CRAIG LABENZ: Now,
here, I usually

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:05.220
start with the
smallest machine that

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:06.900
qualifies for
Google service level

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:12.090
agreement, which is one virtual
CPU and 3.75 gigabytes of RAM.

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:14.490
Now estimating hardware
needs ahead of time

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:16.230
is extremely
difficult for any app,

00:05:16.230 --> 00:05:18.510
especially one not
yet launched, which

00:05:18.510 --> 00:05:21.850
is why we're going to
run some load tests.

00:05:21.850 --> 00:05:23.290
MARTIN OMANDER: [CHUCKLES] Cool.

00:05:23.290 --> 00:05:26.890
So I pick one virtual CPU,
3.75 gigabytes of RAM here.

00:05:26.890 --> 00:05:31.120
But what if I find out that I
chose the wrong size later on?

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:31.630
What then?

00:05:31.630 --> 00:05:32.800
CRAIG LABENZ: Well, if
your app can tolerate

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:34.810
a few minutes of downtime,
more database power

00:05:34.810 --> 00:05:37.090
is never further away than
turning off your instance,

00:05:37.090 --> 00:05:39.430
resizing it, and
turning it back on.

00:05:39.430 --> 00:05:42.010
Of course, if your app
cannot tolerate a few minutes

00:05:42.010 --> 00:05:44.320
of downtime, then the
standard workflow is the same

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:45.340
as changing regions.

00:05:45.340 --> 00:05:47.200
You create a larger
read replica,

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:49.490
wait until it catches
up to production,

00:05:49.490 --> 00:05:50.350
and then promote it.

00:05:50.350 --> 00:05:52.570
Now, next, as for storage
type and encryption,

00:05:52.570 --> 00:05:54.610
I strongly recommend
solid state drives

00:05:54.610 --> 00:05:56.110
and Google-managed encryption.

00:05:56.110 --> 00:05:59.590
Those are easy wins, and
they reduce your headaches.

00:05:59.590 --> 00:06:00.760
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, OK.

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:01.510
Let's see.

00:06:01.510 --> 00:06:04.420
So it looks like
next is Backups.

00:06:04.420 --> 00:06:07.510
Ah, great, looks like
backups are on by default.

00:06:07.510 --> 00:06:10.490
And closing Backups.

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:15.040
Then we have Flags, Maintenance,
Query Insights, Labels.

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:16.720
That sounds like
extra credit, so I

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:18.650
think we might be good to go.

00:06:18.650 --> 00:06:21.040
I'm clicking Create.

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:24.648
CRAIG LABENZ: You've taken your
first step into a larger world.

00:06:24.648 --> 00:06:27.190
But say, we've got a few minutes
while this database instance

00:06:27.190 --> 00:06:28.240
gets created.

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:30.128
Top off coffee?

00:06:30.128 --> 00:06:31.670
MARTIN OMANDER: Ah,
well, tea for me.

00:06:31.670 --> 00:06:34.340
But yeah, that sounds great.

00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:38.120
All right, I refilled my tea,
and my database is created.

00:06:38.120 --> 00:06:40.550
Before we go any further,
I'll use Cloud Shell

00:06:40.550 --> 00:06:42.870
to create all of my tables.

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:45.590
So if I scroll down
here, I can click

00:06:45.590 --> 00:06:46.970
Connect using Cloud Shell.

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:53.880
All right, so I'm
in, and I'm going

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:58.320
to paste in the statements
to create the tables.

00:06:58.320 --> 00:06:59.190
There it is.

00:07:01.970 --> 00:07:02.630
All right.

00:07:02.630 --> 00:07:05.450
So now, my next
question is, what values

00:07:05.450 --> 00:07:08.780
should I use in my code
to connect to my Cloud SQL

00:07:08.780 --> 00:07:10.558
instance?

00:07:10.558 --> 00:07:13.100
CRAIG LABENZ: Well, usually,
you would use your database's IP

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:13.600
address.

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:15.680
But Cloud Run
creates a Unix socket

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:18.620
in your running container
for each connected Cloud SQL

00:07:18.620 --> 00:07:19.370
instance.

00:07:19.370 --> 00:07:21.830
This means all that your
application code has to do

00:07:21.830 --> 00:07:23.900
is to that socket file.

00:07:23.900 --> 00:07:26.030
I sent you a working
code sample earlier.

00:07:26.030 --> 00:07:27.040
Did you get it?

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:28.820
MARTIN OMANDER:
Socket.txt-- yeah, right.

00:07:28.820 --> 00:07:32.100
This is the one you
sent to me, right?

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:32.870
CRAIG LABENZ: Yes.

00:07:32.870 --> 00:07:38.150
So with that, you can copy
that and go back into main.py.

00:07:38.150 --> 00:07:41.810
And you can remove the
host and port arguments,

00:07:41.810 --> 00:07:44.840
and, instead, supply
that copied code

00:07:44.840 --> 00:07:47.540
as the path to your
Unix socket file.

00:07:47.540 --> 00:07:50.450
And like all of our other
database connection values,

00:07:50.450 --> 00:07:55.016
it's best to read that path
from an environment variable.

00:07:55.016 --> 00:08:00.770
Now, in Cloud Run the path is
always /cloudsql/ your database

00:08:00.770 --> 00:08:04.010
connection name, which you can
find on your Cloud SQL instance

00:08:04.010 --> 00:08:04.877
screen.

00:08:04.877 --> 00:08:05.960
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:08:05.960 --> 00:08:10.100
So let's see.

00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:13.880
I have an environment file here.

00:08:13.880 --> 00:08:18.170
I took out host and port.

00:08:18.170 --> 00:08:23.720
So, and in my
database-- ah, right.

00:08:23.720 --> 00:08:24.890
There's the connection name.

00:08:24.890 --> 00:08:25.390
Cool.

00:08:25.390 --> 00:08:26.480
I'll just copy that.

00:08:29.580 --> 00:08:31.090
So that's the connection.

00:08:31.090 --> 00:08:32.507
CRAIG LABENZ: Yeah,
so you can put

00:08:32.507 --> 00:08:33.780
all the values in this file.

00:08:33.780 --> 00:08:37.200
And for the database user, we
use the default Postgres user

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:38.130
of postgres.

00:08:38.130 --> 00:08:40.315
MARTIN OMANDER:
Postgres, got it.

00:08:40.315 --> 00:08:41.940
CRAIG LABENZ: And
I'm sure you remember

00:08:41.940 --> 00:08:43.107
your best-in-class password.

00:08:43.107 --> 00:08:47.100
MARTIN OMANDER: Ah,
yes, uncrackable.

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:49.810
CRAIG LABENZ: And we also use
the default Postgres database,

00:08:49.810 --> 00:08:50.310
very nice.

00:08:50.310 --> 00:08:53.410
MARTIN OMANDER: OK, postgres.

00:08:53.410 --> 00:08:56.380
There we are.

00:08:56.380 --> 00:08:59.890
All right, so I placed
a database username,

00:08:59.890 --> 00:09:05.420
and password, database
name, and connection name.

00:09:05.420 --> 00:09:08.958
CRAIG LABENZ: Now, it looks like
we're ready to deploy your app.

00:09:08.958 --> 00:09:10.750
MARTIN OMANDER: So I
have a script prepared

00:09:10.750 --> 00:09:14.263
that handles the entire
deployment process.

00:09:14.263 --> 00:09:15.930
CRAIG LABENZ: Well,
that sounds amazing.

00:09:15.930 --> 00:09:17.190
Can I see it?

00:09:17.190 --> 00:09:19.420
MARTIN OMANDER: I'll just
start it really quick here.

00:09:19.420 --> 00:09:22.470
So what this does
is, up here, it

00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:24.840
sets three environment
variables that

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:26.340
would be used further down.

00:09:26.340 --> 00:09:27.970
And then it's two steps.

00:09:27.970 --> 00:09:30.600
So the first one is
gcloud builds submit,

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:34.410
which just packages everything
up in this directory

00:09:34.410 --> 00:09:38.640
and sends it to Cloud
Build, a Google build server

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:40.940
that builds the container.

00:09:40.940 --> 00:09:42.950
That's what's
going on right now.

00:09:42.950 --> 00:09:47.330
And then down here
is gcloud run deploy,

00:09:47.330 --> 00:09:51.860
which takes that container and
deploys it as a new Cloud Run

00:09:51.860 --> 00:09:53.480
service.

00:09:53.480 --> 00:09:57.830
Also, it includes here
add instances flag

00:09:57.830 --> 00:09:59.930
that sends along
the connection name

00:09:59.930 --> 00:10:02.930
so that Cloud Run knows
which database to connect to.

00:10:02.930 --> 00:10:03.680
CRAIG LABENZ: Wow.

00:10:03.680 --> 00:10:05.722
And to think, now, you
can deploy your entire app

00:10:05.722 --> 00:10:07.022
with just a few key presses.

00:10:07.022 --> 00:10:08.480
This is really
going to lend itself

00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:10.310
to a nice CI/CD pipeline.

00:10:10.310 --> 00:10:13.280
MARTIN OMANDER: Hey, it looks
like the deploy was a success.

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:14.690
Let's see if it loads.

00:10:14.690 --> 00:10:16.910
I'll go to this.

00:10:16.910 --> 00:10:18.260
You're all here.

00:10:18.260 --> 00:10:21.020
It looks like I've connected
Cloud Run, which is serverless,

00:10:21.020 --> 00:10:24.410
with a traditional SQL database
running on dedicated hardware.

00:10:24.410 --> 00:10:28.550
I can scale it up by adding
CPU memory or read replicas.

00:10:28.550 --> 00:10:30.230
But will I need to do that?

00:10:30.230 --> 00:10:33.160
How much traffic can my
system handle, Craig?

00:10:33.160 --> 00:10:34.910
CRAIG LABENZ: Well all
apps are different.

00:10:34.910 --> 00:10:37.992
And the only way to know
is to run a load test.

00:10:37.992 --> 00:10:39.200
MARTIN OMANDER: Sounds great.

00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:42.470
Stay tuned for in the next
video, where we do that.

00:10:42.470 --> 00:10:46.120
[MUSIC PLAYING]

